  "tree_id": 799,
  "family": "Malvaceae",
  "memo": "Norfolk Island Hibiscus is best known for its striking, bell-shaped pink to mauve flowers, which fade to white over time. This evergreen tree boasts a dense, rounded canopy with olive-green leaves. Its bark is smooth and gray, often becoming furrowed with age. Its brown seed capsules can be quite messy, releasing fluffy fibers that irritate the skin. This tree is well-suited for seaside and urban conditions, as it is highly tolerant of salt and wind, adding a splash of color to these landscapes. It also attracts various pollinators, enhancing the local ecosystem. This species is listed as Near Threatened by the IUCN.&nbsp;",
